\chapter{Function-scoped defer}
\label{ch:29}
\index{Function-scoped defer}
\index{Cleanup!defer}

\OnxCodeTerm{defer call;} snapshots the callable and its arguments at registration. An admitted function drains its deferred calls in last-in, first-out order on its normal return or on a propagated Result error. Leaving a loop body does not drain the surrounding function's defer stack. Defers have bounded registration and capture storage. Fatal traps do not run a general unwinder.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none]
fn main()
{
  var count = 1;
  defer println(count);
  count = 2;
  defer println(count);
  println(3);
}
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

The ordinary call prints 3. The two saved calls print 2 and 1.

\section{Whose function is returning?}
In \OnxFile{on1x/examples/defer_errors.on1x}, three functions have
defers. The loop in \OnxCodeTerm{main} registers prints of 0, 1, and
2. They stay pending while \OnxCodeTerm{main} calls
\OnxCodeTerm{twice(21)}, which registers a print of 99 and calls
\OnxCodeTerm{read(21)}. Read the output from the inside out:
\begin{itemize}
\item \OnxCodeTerm{read} returns \OnxCodeTerm{Ok(21)} and prints its
saved argument, 21, on the way out. Then \OnxCodeTerm{twice} doubles
the payload, saves its result, and prints 99. Only now can the caller
match that result and print 42.
\item With input $-1$, \OnxCodeTerm{read} returns \OnxCodeTerm{Err(7)}
and prints $-1$. The \OnxCodeTerm{?} in \OnxCodeTerm{twice} skips
the multiplication. It still exits the function, so 99 is printed
before the caller prints 7.
\item Finally \OnxCodeTerm{main} returns. Its loop has long since
ended, but its three registrations print 2, 1, and 0, in that order.
\end{itemize}
For an experiment in which a deferred function observes an owner just
before lexical cleanup, see \cref{app:ownership}.

\section{Trace registration separately from invocation}
Imagine a helper which prints its integer argument and returns that
same integer. In the small counter example, put a call to the helper
around the argument of the first deferred print. There should now be
a line containing 1 before the ordinary print of 3: evaluating the
argument runs the helper immediately. The saved print still runs last
and produces another 1. Wrapping the second argument as well gives
the complete sequence 1, 2, 3, 2, 1. This distinguishes saving a
value from saving an expression to be evaluated when the stack drains.

The distinction also determines what happens when a registration
contains a result propagation. If an argument returns early through
a question mark, the deferred call whose arguments were being assembled
has not yet been registered. Earlier, completed registrations in that
function do run on the error return. The partially assembled call
does not run with a missing argument. On an ordinary return the result
expression is likewise evaluated before draining starts. A deferred
function may itself return a value, but that value is discarded rather
than substituted for the result already saved by its caller.

